No general federal law requires a private employer to pay the Internal Revenue Service (IRS) standard mileage rate to W-2 employees. Under the Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA), required vehicle costs cannot push a worker's weekly pay below the federal minimum wage of $7.25 per hour. State rules can also require reimbursement. California Labor Code Section 2802, for example, requires employers to reimburse employees for necessary vehicle costs, as covered under [state mileage reimbursement laws](/guides/state-mileage-reimbursement-law-california-texas-michigan/).

Many employers adopt the IRS rate for convenience because payments up to that level are non-taxable under an accountable plan. If an employer pays more than the IRS rate, generally only the excess is taxed as wages on Form W-2. You can calculate your expected payout with a [mileage reimbursement calculator](/mileage-reimbursement-calculator/).